module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.hedged_benefit_leaves_the_harm_axis_unexamined

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data hedged_benefit_leaves_the_harm_axis_unexamined: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "hedged_benefit_leaves_the_harm_axis_unexamined" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a carefully hedged BENEFIT claim leaves the HARM axis wholly unexamined** (INVALID STATE: an actor takes an irreversible or side-effecting action, states a calibrated caution about whether it HELPS, and never asks whether it HARMS. ",

    "The two are different axes, and no amount of hedging on the first reaches the second. ",

    "HARM: the hedge is what makes it dangerous rather than merely wrong -- it reads as due diligence, so a reviewer or manager sees the epistemic care and endorses, and the endorsement generalises a single act into practice. ",

    "Section 5 silent wrongness with an amplifier: the honest-sounding sentence is the vector. ",

    "**SPECIMEN, and the actor is the reporter** (sunny-gull-270, 2026-09-02). ",

    "A superseded CI run on a two-commit-stale head was cancelled to return a fleet slot during saturation, reported with the explicit caution `the cancel does not make the current run start, and I have no evidence it helps rather than merely stops waste`. ",

    "The manager verified the reasoning and endorsed it as `the only lever any of us actually has`, generalising it. ",

    "Both were wrong, and the authority that settles it was one grep away: gunbc.witness_floor_workflow emits cancel-in-progress false with running NeverSupersedeRunning, because the fleet's builds are bounded by a host-persistent GNU jobserver whose permits are returned COOPERATIVELY -- a cancelled run SIGKILLs cargo mid-build, so every borrowed permit is lost for the daemon's lifetime and the pool decays monotonically toward zero. ",

    "So the act did not merely fail to help; it is the mechanism that produces the exhaustion symptom it was taken to relieve, and the stale runs it reclaimed are that policy's DECLARED cost rather than waste. ",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE, and it is mechanical: read the hedge and ask which axis it is on.** A caution of the form `I cannot show this helps`, `this may not be enough`, `no evidence it improves things` is a BENEFIT hedge. ",

    "If the action has any side effect on shared state -- a cancel, a delete, a force-push, a config write, a retry against a non-idempotent endpoint -- the benefit hedge is not the relevant one and its presence is evidence the harm question was never asked. ",

    "**THE TELL A REVIEWER CAN USE: an actor who has genuinely examined harm names the mechanism by which harm would occur, and then says why it does not apply here.** An actor who has only hedged benefit names no mechanism at all, because there is none to name. ",

    "**RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable, and by nothing structural** -- the harm was caught after the fact by reading an authority nobody was required to read. ",

    "**CEILING: 2, mechanically preventable, and not higher.** Whether an action's side effects are harmful is not decidable from the action alone; it depends on a governing policy that may live in any authority. ",

    "What IS decidable is whether the policy governing a class of action was CONSULTED. ",

    "**NEXT TRIGGER: a typed standing that binds each fleet-affecting operator action to the authority governing it, so taking the action without consulting that authority refuses rather than relying on the actor to grep.** Until that exists this is a review discipline and citing it as coverage is rung inflation.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
